{"title":"2021 AMC 12A Problems/Problem 6","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Solution_1_(Algebra)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 1 (Algebra)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Solution_2_(Arithmetic)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 2 (Arithmetic)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Solution_3_(Observations)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 3 (Observations)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#Solution_4_(Ratios_and_Proportions)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">5</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 4 (Ratios and Proportions)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-6\"><a href=\"#Solution_5_(Simpler_Ratios)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">6</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 5 (Simpler Ratios)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-7\"><a href=\"#Solution_6_(Observation)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">7</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 6 (Observation)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-8\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_(Quick_and_Easy)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">8</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution (Quick and Easy)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-9\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_by_Aaron_He\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">9</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution by Aaron He</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-10\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_by_Hawk_Math\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">10</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution by Hawk Math</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-11\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_by_OmegaLearn_(Using_Probability_and_System_of_Equations)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">11</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution by OmegaLearn (Using Probability and System of Equations)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-12\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_by_TheBeautyofMath\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">12</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution by TheBeautyofMath</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-13\"><a href=\"#See_also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">13</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2021_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_6&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>A deck of cards has only red cards and black cards. The probability of a randomly chosen card being red is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/b/a/ebafcc60a28d8759185d7808c60ea53efc73938e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac13$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"11\" height=\"37\" />. When <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/c/c7cab1a05e1e0c1d51a6a219d96577a16b7abf9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> black cards are added to the deck, the probability of choosing red becomes <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/c/d/4cd0f9f9869b39e79527f9010293adf7bfe5210f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac14$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"15\" height=\"38\" />. How many cards were in the deck originally?\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/8/d/98d1863e4f6778fbad26e150ec612af5bd6b1537.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\textbf{(A) }6 \\qquad \\textbf{(B) }9 \\qquad \\textbf{(C) }12 \\qquad \\textbf{(D) }15 \\qquad \\textbf{(E) }18$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"380\" height=\"17\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_1_.28Algebra.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1_(Algebra)\">Solution 1 (Algebra)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2021_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_6&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1 (Algebra)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>If the probability of choosing a red card is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/e/1/4e1965f8d70832ded66434c75a7bd52b75090af0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{3}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"15\" height=\"38\" />, the red and black cards are in ratio <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/2/e/32e1a1a7adf986532b7598e896785a56c785f779.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1:2$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"32\" height=\"12\" />. This means at the beginning there are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> red cards and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/e/c/2ec701493fa72dda5d6747fe14f30e7b1c2fb6c4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2x$\" width=\"19\" height=\"12\" /> black cards.\n</p><p>After <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/c/c7cab1a05e1e0c1d51a6a219d96577a16b7abf9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> black cards are added, there are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/5/6/556f069a13a66d2df5c5d2673bc44fa7f295bf45.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2x+4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"50\" height=\"13\" /> black cards. This time, the probability of choosing a red card is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/3/c339afa03e060d959e4942be7522c0c992f4c41c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{4}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"15\" height=\"38\" /> so the ratio of red to black cards is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/2/7/627826fdfb63aff2c791d85b922176f0af35c41f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1:3$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"32\" height=\"13\" />. This means in the new deck the number of black cards is also <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/0/e/70e634348f8df9eaeac4ced35db64d08a906532e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3x$\" width=\"19\" height=\"12\" /> for the same <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> red cards.\n</p><p>So, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/b/c/1bcfe53432957206edb571590ce97a3270cf8685.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3x = 2x + 4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"94\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/e/7/2e7d65cfa316d639c9267398009812f3eb45fea9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x=4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"46\" height=\"14\" /> meaning there are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/c/c7cab1a05e1e0c1d51a6a219d96577a16b7abf9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> red cards in the deck at the start and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/1/7/81791fb99a3dbf3195d35a82f7dedb69490a3e5b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2(4) = 8$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"65\" height=\"18\" /> black cards. \n</p><p>So, the answer is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/4/b/44bf059a4c3cf37021bab393cbc997df91ef7a78.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$8+4 = 12 = \\boxed{\\textbf{(C) }12}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"173\" height=\"30\" />.\n</p><p>--abhinavg0627\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_2_.28Arithmetic.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2_(Arithmetic)\">Solution 2 (Arithmetic)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2021_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_6&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2 (Arithmetic)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>In terms of the number of cards, the original deck is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> times the red cards, and the final deck is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/c/c7cab1a05e1e0c1d51a6a219d96577a16b7abf9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> times the red cards. So, the final deck is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/a/b/7ab458a1f23acc24632e0d00177f2b09b29fa92d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac43$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"11\" height=\"37\" /> times the original deck. We are given that adding <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/c/c7cab1a05e1e0c1d51a6a219d96577a16b7abf9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> cards to the original deck is the same as increasing the original deck by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/b/a/ebafcc60a28d8759185d7808c60ea53efc73938e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac13$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"11\" height=\"37\" /> of itself. Since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/c/c7cab1a05e1e0c1d51a6a219d96577a16b7abf9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> cards are equal to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/b/a/ebafcc60a28d8759185d7808c60ea53efc73938e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac13$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"11\" height=\"37\" /> of the original deck, the original deck has <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/9/1/f9180b5ef17d5ba1023e1db768605ad944caf788.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4\\cdot3=\\boxed{\\textbf{(C) }12}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -10px\" width=\"120\" height=\"28\" /> cards.\n</p><p>~MRENTHUSIASM\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_3_.28Observations.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_3_(Observations)\">Solution 3 (Observations)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2021_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_6&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 3 (Observations)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Suppose there were <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> cards in the deck originally. Now, the deck has <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/e/7/ae728897cc815fa29fa3b01affbb9e5592409d2e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x+4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"44\" height=\"15\" /> cards, which must be a multiple of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/b/1/cb15a16cca90d201094265ee529e9e2cfb113de8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />\n</p><p>Only <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26ee312a7e136745b07c69c4c48e5f4e716d1823.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$12+4=16$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"91\" height=\"14\" /> is a multiple of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/0/5/4054552638cf9f29e857a1306bd3ec31df3c19b6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"13\" height=\"15\" /> so the answer is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/e/0/fe058960c13268d583fca6f4f4de67d8994b9e23.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x=\\boxed{\\textbf{(C) }12}.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -10px\" width=\"102\" height=\"28\" />\n</p><p>~MRENTHUSIASM\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_4_.28Ratios_and_Proportions.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_4_(Ratios_and_Proportions)\">Solution 4 (Ratios and Proportions)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2021_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_6&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 4 (Ratios and Proportions)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>By looking at the ratio of black cards to the total number of cards, we can say that there are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/e/c/2ec701493fa72dda5d6747fe14f30e7b1c2fb6c4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2x$\" width=\"19\" height=\"12\" /> black cards and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/0/e/70e634348f8df9eaeac4ced35db64d08a906532e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3x$\" width=\"19\" height=\"12\" /> cards in total. We can write the probability of getting a black card as <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/6/8c6f1c6fc45c0d4271969bc3ae2e0ffcaadb0292.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{2x}{3x}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"25\" height=\"38\" />. But when we increase the number of black cards by 4 the probability becomes <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/1/1/e1150a17bf1b733cb666be84d920c71fc96bb3f7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{3}{4}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"11\" height=\"38\" />. So the equation is:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/3/d/73d45ba06a82d15013f13dbd7efe3390d09457df.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{2x+4}{3x+4}=\\frac{3}{4}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -15px\" width=\"94\" height=\"40\" />,\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/5/d/05dc0e7aaac8b314d2030d7bbbf87f6d5abb67f4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$8x+16=9x+12$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"146\" height=\"15\" />,\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/e/7/2e7d65cfa316d639c9267398009812f3eb45fea9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x=4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"46\" height=\"14\" />.\nThe answer is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/4/3/1435f6292031f54db1f4a8bd66b1c45fc7187e6e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3x=\\boxed{\\textbf{(C) }12}.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"115\" height=\"30\" />\n</p><p>~Param Gor\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_5_.28Simpler_Ratios.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_5_(Simpler_Ratios)\">Solution 5 (Simpler Ratios)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2021_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_6&amp;action=edit&amp;section=6\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 5 (Simpler Ratios)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>This could in a way be considered a more efficient/alternate version of Solution 4. Since the problem mentions that there is initially a <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/e/1/4e1965f8d70832ded66434c75a7bd52b75090af0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{3}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"15\" height=\"38\" /> chance of getting a red card, we can represent the number of red cards as <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/3/f/a3fc48324430ab8e4592260bcaccc655503255f8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{x}{3}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"16\" height=\"34\" />, where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> is the number of cards in the deck originally. Since the modification to the deck makes no changes to the number of red cards, you can see that if there is a <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/3/c339afa03e060d959e4942be7522c0c992f4c41c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{4}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"15\" height=\"38\" /> probability of having a red card in a deck of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/e/7/ae728897cc815fa29fa3b01affbb9e5592409d2e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x+4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"44\" height=\"15\" /> cards, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/2/2/322d47770a7239dcd37f9a27285bfcf51c75ceaf.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{x}{3} = \\frac{x+4}{4}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"86\" height=\"38\" />. Solving this for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> gives <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/e/0/fe058960c13268d583fca6f4f4de67d8994b9e23.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x=\\boxed{\\textbf{(C) }12}.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -10px\" width=\"102\" height=\"28\" />\n</p><p>~Almond_Oil\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_6_.28Observation.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_6_(Observation)\">Solution 6 (Observation)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2021_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_6&amp;action=edit&amp;section=7\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 6 (Observation)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Notice that in a deck with 1 red card and 2 black cards, the probability of a red ball is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/e/1/4e1965f8d70832ded66434c75a7bd52b75090af0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{3}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"15\" height=\"38\" />\n</p><p>Adding a single black card would make the chance <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/3/c339afa03e060d959e4942be7522c0c992f4c41c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{4}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"15\" height=\"38\" />\n</p><p>If we scale this deck by 4, making so that there are 4 red cards and 8 black cards, the original chance is still <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/e/1/4e1965f8d70832ded66434c75a7bd52b75090af0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{3}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"15\" height=\"38\" />\n</p><p>Adding 4 black cards will make the chance <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/3/c339afa03e060d959e4942be7522c0c992f4c41c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{4}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"15\" height=\"38\" />\n</p><p>The answer is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/5/7/2576b9442cb4b0e6cfb195f24ece0e6698f38a80.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\textbf{(C) }12}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -10px\" width=\"65\" height=\"28\" />.\n</p><p><br />\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Video_Solution_.28Quick_and_Easy.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_(Quick_and_Easy)\">Video Solution (Quick and Easy)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2021_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_6&amp;action=edit&amp;section=8\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution (Quick and Easy)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://youtu.be/oYGeTN2-82s\">https://youtu.be/oYGeTN2-82s</a>\n</p><p>~Education, the Study of Everything\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_by_Aaron_He\">Video Solution by Aaron He</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2021_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_6&amp;action=edit&amp;section=9\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution by Aaron He\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=xTGDKBthWsw&amp;t=5m51s\">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=xTGDKBthWsw&amp;t=5m51s</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_by_Hawk_Math\">Video Solution by Hawk Math</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2021_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_6&amp;action=edit&amp;section=10\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution by Hawk Math\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=P5al76DxyHY\">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=P5al76DxyHY</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Video_Solution_by_OmegaLearn_.28Using_Probability_and_System_of_Equations.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_by_OmegaLearn_(Using_Probability_and_System_of_Equations)\">Video Solution by OmegaLearn (Using Probability and System of Equations)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2021_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_6&amp;action=edit&amp;section=11\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution by OmegaLearn (Using Probability and System of Equations)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://youtu.be/C6x361JPLzU\">https://youtu.be/C6x361JPLzU</a>\n</p><p>~ pi_is_3.14\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_by_TheBeautyofMath\">Video Solution by TheBeautyofMath</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2021_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_6&amp;action=edit&amp;section=12\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution by TheBeautyofMath\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://youtu.be/cckGBU2x1zg\">https://youtu.be/cckGBU2x1zg</a>\n</p><p>~IceMatrix\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_also\">See also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2021_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_6&amp;action=edit&amp;section=13\" title=\"Edit section: See also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black; width:80%;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"2\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2021_AMC_12A\" title=\"2021 AMC 12A\">2021 AMC 12A</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2021_AMC_12A_Problems\" title=\"2021 AMC 12A Problems\">Problems</a></b> • <b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2021_AMC_12A_Answer_Key\" title=\"2021 AMC 12A Answer Key\">Answer Key</a></b> • <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"http://www.artofproblemsolving.com/community/c13\">Resources</a>)\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Preceded&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2021_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_5\" title=\"2021 AMC 12A Problems/Problem 5\">Problem 5</a></b>\n</td>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Followed&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2021_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_7\" title=\"2021 AMC 12A Problems/Problem 7\">Problem 7</a></b>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2021_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_1\" title=\"2021 AMC 12A Problems/Problem 1\">1</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2021_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_2\" title=\"2021 AMC 12A Problems/Problem 2\">2</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2021_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_3\" title=\"2021 AMC 12A Problems/Problem 3\">3</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2021_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_4\" title=\"2021 AMC 12A Problems/Problem 4\">4</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2021_AMC_1